Here's an example C++ function that accepts a reference to an std::string containing a text in reduced (en-dash) format and returns an std::string containing the same text in HTML format with en-dash replaced by &ndash;:

```cpp
#include <string>

std::string reduceToHtml(const std::string& input) {
    std::string output = input;
    size_t pos = 0;
    while ((pos = output.find("--")) != std::string::npos) {
        output.replace(pos, 2, "&ndash;");
    }
    return output;
}
```

Example usage:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::string input = "This is a -- reduced string.";
    std::string output = reduceToHtml(input);
    std::cout << output << std::endl; // Output: This is a &ndash; reduced string.
    return 0;
}
``` 

Note: This implementation replaces only double hyphen (`--`) with en-dash (`&ndash;`) in the input string. If you need to handle other types of dashes or any other formatting, you'll need to modify the function accordingly.

